<%# 010 FR-001: where the user's locker wish stands, on the page they land on.
    Three mutually exclusive states — the wish they declared, or one of two
    invitations to declare one — so the block never offers two ways in and
    never offers none (SC-002). The wish wins over both invitations: someone
    who has already said what they are looking for is not asked again.

    Read through current_user rather than an instance variable. The homepage has
    two renderers — HomeController, and LockerProfilesController re-rendering a
    rejected locker edit — and a variable only one of them sets would drop this
    block exactly when a user has just made a mistake (research.md §1).

    Every state links to the wish page rather than acting here: declaring,
    changing and cancelling all live there, and this block only reports and
    points (FR-009). %>
<div id="home-locker-wish" class="card card--you stack-tight">
  <% if current_user.locker_wish&.persisted? %>
    <%# The heading the wish page's own panel carries, deliberately: the same card
        title for the same thing on both screens is continuity, not repetition. %>
    <h2 class="card-title"><%= t(".title") %></h2>

    <dl class="detail-inline">
      <dt class="detail-term"><%= t(".looking_on_floor") %></dt>
      <%# FR-002: the floor sought, and nothing else. What this user already has
          is on the card immediately below — saying it twice would be two copies
          of one fact, free to disagree. The value on file rather than the
          attribute, for the same reason the card below reads past it. %>
      <dd id="home-locker-wish-floor" class="detail-value"><%= current_user.locker_wish.saved_floor %></dd>
    </dl>

    <%# 021: no .row wrapper. .row is a flex row, so a button inside it hugs its
        own text; as a direct child of the card's own column it stretches to the
        card — which is exactly what "Save locker details" does inside its
        form.stack-tight. The two match because they are laid out the same way,
        not because a width was pinned to make them agree. %>
    <%# A link, not a button_to: this navigates and changes nothing, so it is
        announced as a link and the back button behaves (research.md §2). %>
    <%= link_to t(".review_wishes"), locker_wishes_path, class: "btn btn-primary btn-arrow" %>
  <% elsif current_user.saved_locker_number.present? %>
    <%# FR-004: a locker on file and nothing said about it. The heading asks the
        question the button answers, rather than calling this "your locker
        search" when no search exists yet.

        FR-013: nothing here consults the swap proposals. An accepted one freezes
        the card below — those values are what the other side agreed to — but a
        wish says what someone wants, which no proposal has any claim on. %>
    <h2 class="card-title"><%= t(".different_locker_title") %></h2>

    <p class="card-lead">
      <%= t(".different_locker_lead") %>
    </p>

    <%# 021: no .row wrapper. .row is a flex row, so a button inside it hugs its
        own text; as a direct child of the card's own column it stretches to the
        card — which is exactly what "Save locker details" does inside its
        form.stack-tight. The two match because they are laid out the same way,
        not because a width was pinned to make them agree. %>
    <%= link_to t(".switch_locker"), locker_wishes_path, class: "btn btn-primary btn-arrow" %>
  <% else %>
    <%# FR-005, FR-011: no locker, and nothing declared. Having nothing to trade
        is the reason to be here, not a reason to be left out.

        This state is a button like the other two, deliberately. Stated as a bare
        line of text it would leave the person with the least to lose by swapping
        with no way off this page — so the invitation is the control. %>
    <h2 class="card-title"><%= t(".no_locker_title") %></h2>

    <p class="card-lead">
      <%= t(".no_locker_lead") %>
    </p>

    <%# 021: no .row wrapper. .row is a flex row, so a button inside it hugs its
        own text; as a direct child of the card's own column it stretches to the
        card — which is exactly what "Save locker details" does inside its
        form.stack-tight. The two match because they are laid out the same way,
        not because a width was pinned to make them agree. %>
    <%= link_to t(".want_a_locker"), locker_wishes_path, class: "btn btn-primary btn-arrow" %>
  <% end %>
</div>
